# ADR-2607162331: kotoba-lang/dtn gains automatic TURN allocation/permission refresh — a :turn-relay node stays reachable indefinitely instead of silently expiring

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-16
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki

## Problem

ADR-2607162304's own Consequences section named, as an explicitly disclosed
scope reduction, that `kotoba.dtn.transport.udp`'s `:turn-relay` NAT
traversal made only a single Allocate/CreatePermission call at startup —
\"a relay allocated at startup will silently expire after TURN's default
~5-minute lifetime — a real limitation for any long-running deployment.\"
Having just closed the whole ADR series' originating gap and declared its
\"実際に稼働可能\" (actually-operational) claim, this specific limitation
directly undercut that claim for any real, non-trivially-short-lived usage.
This ADR closes it.

## Decision

### Decision 1: periodic refresh, cadence derived from the server's actual grant, not a hardcoded assumption

`schedule-turn-refresh!` reads the LIFETIME actually returned by the
original Allocate response (`granted-lifetime-s`) rather than assuming
RFC 8656's 600s default holds — refreshing at roughly half that granted
value, a conventional TURN client practice. Both the allocation itself
(refreshed via a real signed STUN Refresh request, reusing
`turn_relay.cljc`'s existing message-construction helpers, extended for
this new request type) and the peer permission (independently
shorter-lived per RFC 8656 §9, refreshed via a fresh CreatePermission call
on the same schedule) are kept alive. A single failed refresh attempt is
logged and retried on the next interval rather than crashing the node —
explicitly scoped as \"no exponential backoff, no give-up-after-N-failures\"
in the docstring, a production-hardening increment left for later, not
silently assumed solved.

### Decision 2: an asymmetry in `org-ietf-turn`'s own listener discovered while designing the test — recorded, not worked around invisibly

Reading `kotoba.turn.listener`'s source directly (not assumed): the
Allocate handler ignores any client-requested LIFETIME on the *original*
Allocate request entirely, always granting the fixed 600s default. The
Refresh handler, in contrast, genuinely honors a client-requested
LIFETIME. This asymmetry meant Scenario 4 (below) could not shrink the
literal initial grant to make a realistic-length automated test window —
so it uses the Refresh path (the exact feature under test, not a
workaround around it) to bring a real, live allocation down to a short,
server-granted 3-second window on its first scheduled tick, then proves
continued scheduling keeps it alive past that real deadline. This is
STRONGER than an artificial short-Allocate-window test would have been:
it exercises the actual refresh mechanism a production node would use,
not a special-cased short-lived-Allocate-only test path.

### Decision 3: a genuine negative control, not merely \"no exception was thrown\"

Demo Scenario 4b runs the identical short-window scenario with refresh
scheduling disabled after the same manual shrink-to-3-seconds, waits the
same real 5.5 seconds, and confirms the SERVER's own expiry sweep drops
the allocation (`org-ietf-turn`'s own listener logs `TURN-LISTENER expiry
sweep dropped 1 allocation(s)` — the server's log, not the client's) and
that a subsequent send genuinely fails to arrive. This directly answers
the question a skeptical reader of Scenario 4a's \"success\" would ask —
*would that send have worked anyway, refresh or not?* — with a real,
independently-observable counterfactual failure, not an assumption.

## Verification

- `clojure -M:test` → 45 tests / 226 assertions (was 212; +14 new for `turn_relay.cljc`'s `build-refresh-request`/`response-lifetime`), 0 failures, 0 errors. Independently re-run from a cold clone.
- `clojure -M:lint` → 0 errors, 0 warnings. Independently re-run.
- `nbb --classpath \"src:test:../phone/src:../html/src:../css/src:../wire/src:../bytes/src:../org-ietf-turn/src\" test/kotoba/dtn/transport/udp_turn_demo.cljs` → `RESULT: 4/4 scenarios passed`, exit 0. Scenarios 1-3 (from ADR-2607162304) unmodified and still passing. New Scenario 4a: a real `js/setTimeout`-based 5.5-second wait, no mocked clock, past a real 3-second server-granted window, with a message genuinely still delivered afterward. New Scenario 4b: the same window with refresh disabled, the server's own log confirming a genuine expiry sweep, and the equivalent send genuinely failing to arrive. Independently re-run from a cold clone by the orchestrating session — output identical to the implementing agent's report, including the server-side `TURN-LISTENER expiry sweep dropped 1 allocation(s)` line.
- `dtn`'s other two demos (7-scenario TCP transport, 3-scenario discovery) independently re-run and confirmed unaffected.
- `org-ietf-turn` confirmed completely untouched (`git status --short` empty on a fresh independent clone) — this ADR's design relied on reading, not modifying, its listener's actual behavior (Decision 2).
- Pushed to `github.com/kotoba-lang/dtn` (public), commit `2235e2fb2d8d12c27a37c8be4e3b85b3d0fa4bc3`.
- `manifest/west.yml`: pin advanced via `--entry dtn` (1-line diff, `838910679487→2235e2fb2d8d`, `verify-west-pins: 1 件の pin 変更をすべて検証 OK`), landed via an isolated sibling-path worktree + GitHub API server-side merge, matching every prior ADR in this series.

## Consequences

- The specific, named limitation from ADR-2607162304's own Consequences
  (\"a relay allocated at startup will silently expire... a real limitation
  for any long-running deployment\") is closed — a `:turn-relay`-configured
  `dtn` node now stays reachable for as long as the process runs, not just
  for the first ~5-10 minutes.
- The verification method for this fix (Decision 2/3) is itself a small
  methodological point worth carrying forward: when a server's behavior is
  uncertain, read the actual source rather than assuming RFC defaults
  hold, and when proving \"X prevents Y,\" include a negative control that
  demonstrates Y actually happens without X — not just that X doesn't
  crash.
- Still explicitly scoped down, unchanged from ADR-2607162304's own
  disclosed list plus this ADR's own Decision 1 addition: no exponential
  backoff/give-up logic on repeated refresh failure, no dynamic
  cadence adjustment if a server ever granted an unusually short initial
  lifetime (this ADR's fixed half-of-granted-lifetime cadence assumes the
  initial grant is reasonably long, which held for `org-ietf-turn`'s fixed
  600s default per Decision 2 — a server granting, say, a 2-second initial
  lifetime would need a correspondingly faster cadence than \"half of 2
  seconds\" might comfortably allow for network round-trip time; not a
  concern for the server this was verified against, but named as a
  boundary condition future work should be aware of).
- This is likely the last item this ADR series will close under its own
  originating scope — remaining possibilities (full ICE, IPv6, long-term
  credentials, DoS/quota limits, production retry/backoff) are genuine
  production-hardening increments, not gaps in what this series set out to
  prove (\"is an independent, wire-compatible telecom substrate actually
  operational\"), and would be a new, separately-scoped decision if pursued.

## Alternatives considered

- **Shrinking the original Allocate request's LIFETIME to make Scenario 4
  testable in short real time**, instead of using the Refresh path.
  Rejected once Decision 2's finding (the listener ignores a
  client-requested LIFETIME on Allocate) was made — not a design choice so
  much as a discovered constraint, but recorded because the resulting test
  design (exercising Refresh, the actual feature under test, rather than a
  hypothetical short-Allocate path) turned out to be the stronger proof
  anyway.
- **A vacuous Scenario 4** (just wait and confirm no exception) instead of
  a real negative control. Rejected: this whole ADR series has
  consistently valued non-vacuous proofs (ADR-2607162202's Decision 4 made
  the identical point about a mesh topology's own transport-layer
  exclusion making a naive self-filtering check vacuous) — the 5.5-second
  wait's significance is only provable by showing the counterfactual
  genuinely fails, which Scenario 4b does.
- **Full production retry/backoff on refresh failure** as part of this
  pass. Rejected for now: log-and-retry-next-interval is sufficient to
  close the specific named gap (silent permanent expiry); backoff/give-up
  logic is a real but separable hardening increment, named explicitly as
  still open rather than bundled in partially-done.

